cian","img":null,"gender":"M","born":"1952-01-01","died":null,"birthplace":"Forécariah","parties":[],"education":[],"occupations":["economist","politician","minister"],"wiki":"Mohamed Said Fofana"},"Q21855433":{"id":"Q21855433","name":"Mamady Youla","desc":"Guinean politician and economist","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/20_March_2017_-_Ms_Arancha_Gonz%C3%A1lez_meets_H.E._Mr._Mamady_Youla%2C_Prime_Minister_of_Guinea_(cropped).jpg/330px-20_March_2017_-_Ms_Arancha_Gonz%C3%A1lez_meets_H.E._Mr._Mamady_Youla%2C_Prime_Minister_of_Guinea_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1961-11-05","died":null,"birthplace":"Conakry","parties":["independent politician"],"education":["Félix Houphouët Boigny University"],"occupations":["politician","economist","minister","head of government"],"wiki":"Mamady Youla"},"Q5984807":{"id":"Q5984807","name":"Ibrahima Kassory Fofana","desc":"Prime Minister of Guinea (2018–2021)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Ibrahima_Kassory_Fofana.jpg/330px-Ibrahima_Kassory_Fofana.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1954-04-15","died":null,"birthplace":"Forécariah","parties":[],"education":["American University"],"occupations":["politician","statesperson","official","minister"],"wiki":"Ibrahima Kassory Fofana"},"Q108418519":{"id":"Q108418519","name":"Mamadi Doumbouya","desc":"Guinean military officer and politician (1980-)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Mamadi_Doumbouya_2022.jpg/330px-Mamadi_Doumbouya_2022.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1984-12-05","died":null,"birthplace":"Kankan","parties":[],"education":["École de guerre"],"occupations":["military personnel","politician","military officer"],"wiki":"Mamady Doumbouya"},"Q66708545":{"id":"Q66708545","name":"Mohamed Béavogui","desc":"Prime minister of Guinea","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Mohamed_B%C3%A9avogui_2022.jpg/330px-Mohamed_B%C3%A9avogui_2022.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1953-08-15","died":null,"birthplace":"Porédaka","parties":[],"education":["University of Conakry","Peter the Great Saint Petersburg State Polytechnical University","John F. Kennedy School of Government"],"occupations":["diplomat","politician","international civil servant"],"wiki":"Mohamed Béavogui"},"Q109296984":{"id":"Q109296984","name":"Bernard Goumou","desc":"Politician from Guinea","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Bernard_Goumou.jpg/330px-Bernard_Goumou.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1980-09-08","died":null,"birthplace":"Abidjan","parties":[],"education":[],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Bernard Goumou"},"Q22209348":{"id":"Q22209348","name":"Bah Oury","desc":"Guinean politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Bah_Oury.jpg/330px-Bah_Oury.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1958-03-00","died":null,"birthplace":"Pita","parties":["Union of Democratic Forces of Guinea"],"education":[],"occupations":["politician","minister"],"wiki":"Bah Oury"}},"summary":"Guinea, officially the Republic of Guinea, is a coastal country located in West Africa. It borders the Atlantic Ocean to the west, Guinea-Bissau to the northwest, Senegal to the north, Mali to the northeast, Ivory Coast to the southeast, and Sierra Leone and Liberia to the south. It is sometimes referred to as Guinea-Conakry, after its capital Conakry, to distinguish it from other territories in the eponymous region, such as Guinea-Bissau and Equatorial Guinea. At the census held on 1 July 2025, Guinea had a population of over 17.5 million and an area of 245,857 square kilometres (94,926 sq mi).\nFormerly French Guinea, it achieved independence in October 2, 1958. Guinea has a history of military coups d'état. After decades of authoritarian rule, it held its first democratic election in 2010. As it continued to hold multi-party elections, the country still faces ethnic conflicts, corruption, and abuses by the military and police. In 2011, the United States government claimed that torture by security forces and abuse of women and children (including female genital mutilation) were ongoing human rights issues. In 2021, a military faction overthrew president Alpha Condé and suspended the constitution.\nGuinea is a member of United Nations, ECOWAS, South Atlantic Peace and Cooperation Zone, Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, La Francophonie, and African Union. Its membership in the African Union was suspended in 2021 because of the 2021 coup, but it was reinstated in January 2026. Muslims represent 90% of the population. The country is divided into four geographic regions: Maritime Guinea on the Atlantic coast, the Fouta Djallon or Middle Guinea highlands, the Upper Guinea savannah region in the northeast, and the Guinée forestière region of tropical forests. More than 24 indigenous languages are spoken, and the largest are Susu, Pular, and Maninka, which dominate respectively in Maritime Guinea, Fouta Djallon, and Upper Guinea, while Forest Guinea is ethnolinguistically diverse. Despite that, French only is the language of communication in schools, government administration, and the media. Guinea's economy is mostly dependent on agriculture and mineral production. It is the world's second-largest producer of bauxite and has deposits of diamonds and gold. As of the most recent survey in 2018, 66.2% of the population is affected by multidimensional poverty, and an additional 16.4% are vulnerable to it.","history":"The modern state of Guinea did not come into existence until 1958, but the history of the area stretches back well before European colonization. Its current boundaries were determined by the Berlin Conference (1884–1885) and the French, who ruled Guinea until 1958.","historyTitle":"History of Guinea","curated":null,"indicators":{"population":[[1960,3556750],[1961,3619550],[1962,3684270],[1963,3750950],[1964,3819720],[1965,3890720],[1966,3964160],[1967,4040150],[1968,4118550],[1969,4198300],[1970,4277540],[1971,4355200],[1972,44312